AB  - Soil moisture significantly affects plant growth. Satellites such as SMAP and SMOS estimate global soil moisture, but their coarse resolution limits the usability. Downscaling with higher-resolution satellites improves spatial detail. However, the accuracy assessment remains challenging, particularly in Africa due to limited ground truth data. This study evaluates the accuracy of SMAP/Sentinel-1 downscaling soil moisture products in Ghana using data from the International Soil Moisture Network. The downscaling products are compared with in situ measurements from 21 meteorological stations (2021-2022). Ghana's climate varies from humid rainforest in the south to dry savanna in the north. Seasonal changes in soil moisture were indicative of the characteristics of the climate. Although 9-km SMAP-derived soil moisture was overestimated near water bodies, downscaling (1 and 3 km) improves the accuracy. In addition, accuracy was high in northern areas where the grassland and cropland were dominant, but low in southern areas where the forests were dominant. Surface soil moisture could be estimated with higher accuracy in land covers with less vegetation. Downscaled soil moisture product would be useful for Ghanaian farmers.
