AB  - The soil consists of organic mixture and mineral components, with air and water forming the spaces between them. To keep the soil nutrients within the limits of Field Potential and Essential nutrients and thus optimize crop production, productivity, and profitability, crop water needs can be assessed and irrigation regulated by measuring soil moisture. Emerging prediction models must enhance prediction performance, such as generalization, prediction accuracy, and multi-purpose processing capability. This paper introduces the implementation of classification algorithms like a Decision tree and Naive Bayes, such as hydrological and remote sensing data, to generate precise predictions of soil moisture using statistical evidence. The results show that soil moisture prediction for polluted soil, the Decision tree is efficient and useful. Its good ability to match and generalize data can enrich the system parameters while ensuring high predictive accuracy soil moisture data patterns and values, providing an important theoretical basis for moisture conservation and management of scarcity.
