AB  - Application of regression models through remote sensing for estimating biophysical parameters of crops is one of the key elements for precision agriculture studies. Numerically, this problem is solved separately for each biophysical parameter such as leaf area index, soil moisture, crop height and etc. However, this approach ignores tight relationship among the biophysical parameters, which is essential for driving estimation performance with a limited number of in-situ measurements. As an alternative strategy, a multi-output regression, which also learns the relationship among biophysical parameters in the regression model, is considered. In order to see how multi-output regression models capture the plausible physical relationship between crops biophysical parameters and polarimetric features, RadarSAT-2 images acquired over agriculture fields in the context of the AgriSAR 2009 campaign were used. Specifically, multi output Gaussian Processes and multi-output Support Vector Machines, which arc two powerful kernel-based methods, are implemented and assessed in the context of accuracy assessment of the biophysical parameter estimation.
