AB  - Drought, which implies a prolonged dry period, can occur anywhere in the world and has devastating impacts on agriculture, the economy, and the environment. Monitoring drought helps reduce disaster risk and improve the livelihood of drought-prone communities. The previous studies on agricultural drought in Bangladesh were on limited areas and earlier periods; the latest drought pattern during the Rabi cropping season in the country is essential to analyze. This study monitored the spatiotemporal variation of agricultural droughts from 2000 to 2022 using the Vegetation Condition Index (VCI) and Vegetation Health Index (VHI). The indices indicating the droughts were determined for the Rabi cropping season (mid-October to mid-March). The two indices, VCI and VHI, exhibited a positive correlation when tested using in situ soil moisture data. Analysis of the VCI revealed that the highest extremes are found at 34.33%, 25.50%, and 20.78% areas in 2000, 2002, and 2008, respectively; whereas the VHI results show 32.46% and 16.51% areas in 2000, and 2008, respectively. Most of the areas of the country noticed no droughts from 2010 to 2022. This may be attributed to the development of irrigation facilities throughout the country. Both the indices indicate that the northern region is more susceptible than the southern region. The findings of this study will be useful to authorities in utilizing remote sensing-based indices for drought monitoring and preparing for drought-related challenges.
